#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105575 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "d044f208-be6c-508f-b92d-221f36a8edcf" :question/math-academy-id "e-7352" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped $6$ times\n\n- The number of times a $6$-sided die is rolled until a $6$ appears\n\n- The number of months in a particular year" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,\\ldots$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of months in a particular year is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $12$ months in a year.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105576 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53675175-8261-54ef-8dfb-2b3087b91239" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Probability Mass Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "528a4e6c-6338-56ae-a56b-3de96b69b32c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7353" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/9e8f3f1bb50432add6ba6c1b91fb5e1f7ab052e48ab155c2e92b40bc309e81a5.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/9363d37d53374519f1170d2c1c24ecb690a6810a304207ef21a0d1f3eba609de.png) to be a valid probability mass function on a set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/5c3d9b70452a08b50ba5b826f5694c3d85c999754e5304ff71d47362a66534cd.png) it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/53ad6d309b94b7889b85ecc5d90ae0201d2ba7b25b2b82cc9b6c95d1165ba268.png) for all ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/86d18b1a03e111c64a7328566ad27a5ae12870cf2ee67e9750f5747bee71eb86.png) in ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/d4aaadce376b13dc707757d84b05abf9c95d436aa2e4bea8fb19a078eff136c6.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/024a7717c9fccd8dceb278a0a50934b514706a425b43f76ee9d229b9008b822b.png)\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/06a568b15033fbfbfc33c59b4f4c1b9382df53efb53025fa9bdf5714972c7440.png) is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/d14540db55ffd098075a55e4c744e9cde80545517df1456959c8baea7500586e.png) does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/637d7cec1da56f96138c07b108310c3d842ddc13b466234f78b4ee1757882a1b.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/5d9e7c41ca12f816fdb84db36a35ba66280982891484cbee9db4a7222c863d92.png)).\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/3d7612998c8458ff973fe19d9fa000175d6ab8ce6d2689d593852f0311e73b6b.png) is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/49f6627729021cf5e3e78f4dcbedb957495a385bccdb4484e5a5ac6f892b0161.png) do not sum to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/19ef2d374cfb64bc575f920e7413112e512fdda510e0fa71dfbd8297cf05d96d.png)\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/cb3ebaf420b7fc49cf896463ad37b95855da99cc6356e4795dc81df2d76f5503.png)\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/70bff1a95629c275ea93a10e6130b14e6565bb70465a778e1bff38526ad389da.png) is a valid probability distribution. Indeed, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/5392675a2894798e20c813a2bc0611940d9f37373f85ed4e474016d673d9a5e3.png)\nand\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/10c830df4267667cf3475f1a41b45d5ec27949c1395b00b7502325481dc93830.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/3d8cbf1521bc8f20e8ad2dff7bf427089ee3e1e591cdba51102049daab5d70f5.png) only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105577 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22f7889-e5e4-5237-bb3b-fe1572ef4fbf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within an Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "f70939b9-5f06-57e8-ac4a-d4217a12aa6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7354" :question/problem "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left( X \\leq 3 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(x\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35193ec5-030d-5c78-b536-08e23bd3b7d4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within a Set of Values"}] :question/id #uuid "c27e7286-1b80-532a-945e-e7897f4635eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-7355" :question/problem "| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left(X\\in \\{2,4,6\\}\\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\in {2,4,6}) & =P(X=2)+P(X=4)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(4)+f(6) \\\\  & =0.1+0.1+0.2 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53675175-8261-54ef-8dfb-2b3087b91239" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Probability Mass Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c25dc7-2ab9-5a90-a94d-53f0652659af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359687} {:answer/id #uuid "f28b16a2-7c1b-5f04-a88b-1537c4b3bc28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359688} {:answer/id #uuid "7979cde7-f8ca-513e-8abf-26f5ab52c463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$, $g$ and $h$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359689} {:answer/id #uuid "95dfdedb-60cd-518f-ac53-dd488bd18257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359690} {:answer/id #uuid "845f7ec2-b6d3-59f6-aa49-1f698853ebc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "845f7ec2-b6d3-59f6-aa49-1f698853ebc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359691} :answer-field/id #uuid "977c071a-217a-56f5-afaa-79ec0b195d60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbba20f1-3fd6-53dd-bbd5-acc5b1346e12" :question/math-academy-id "q-124711" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}, & x=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}, & x=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}, & x=\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{25}, & x=0 \\\\ \\frac{23}{25}, & x=1\\end{aligned}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0\\le f(x)\\le 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x)=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $f$ do not sum to $1:$\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}\\ne 1\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}=1.\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{2}{25},\\frac{23}{25}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{25}+\\frac{23}{25}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22f7889-e5e4-5237-bb3b-fe1572ef4fbf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8859a452-6bf9-5f87-8b76-109fdf8eaaba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359694} {:answer/id #uuid "a1053cc8-bf42-57a7-a932-82698df64f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359695} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4ada68-2e5b-500b-8faf-85a72cb697c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359696} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cc1fc4-8dbc-59c7-93b2-7cf7ee658d5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359697} {:answer/id #uuid "284f8df9-bb8d-5003-8e4c-f2fdcae71a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "284f8df9-bb8d-5003-8e4c-f2fdcae71a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359698} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e76af7d-fb53-5f48-9f27-d1e0e138abac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67a641ab-a796-5b3f-92ff-2343fdf8205a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124767" :question/problem "| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n\nCompute $P(X\\le 0)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 0) & =P(X\\in {-2,-1,0}) \\\\  & =P(X=-2)+P(X=-1)+P(X=0) \\\\  & =f(-2)+f(-1)+f(0) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.2 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22f7889-e5e4-5237-bb3b-fe1572ef4fbf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82aa95eb-720a-56b6-8750-2fc189fecbb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82aa95eb-720a-56b6-8750-2fc189fecbb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359701} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e3c63fc-6c15-5438-839c-0b59df63a402" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "256ecdb7-97b3-5497-9f65-c12d66d08b97" :question/math-academy-id "q-323123" :question/problem "| $y$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n\nCompute $P(-1<Y\\le 1)$ given that the random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y)$ shown in the table above.\n\n$P(-1<Y\\le 1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<Y\\le 1) & =P(Y\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =P(Y=0)+P(Y=1) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53675175-8261-54ef-8dfb-2b3087b91239" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Probability Mass Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17152e4f-a665-57ad-9f49-12c0d6af8cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359703} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bdecde-ec15-5b7e-b59e-c2e544a434e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359704} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa68b45-96c4-5c5d-83c5-9111e394772c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359705} {:answer/id #uuid "672c05fe-2724-5a90-994f-2c91c67be61b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359706} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd6a55d-c790-58ef-a88d-47e21b766ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6bdecde-ec15-5b7e-b59e-c2e544a434e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359704} :answer-field/id #uuid "88b28b6d-8989-5f3e-bace-59302c35074b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "732d158b-710d-5722-b1ed-0167e4e3b912" :question/math-academy-id "q-83456" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & x=3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & x=3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}, & x=3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0\\le f(x)\\le 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x)=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability mass function because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $f$ do not sum to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{7}{6}\\ne 1\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=1.\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value $h(3)=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35193ec5-030d-5c78-b536-08e23bd3b7d4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within a Set of Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5858dbb-10f9-5bd9-8694-b6dfce860b63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105084} {:answer/id #uuid "064366ef-7d3f-5699-9915-2b598c22cb26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105085} {:answer/id #uuid "11146513-ee02-52e2-a190-e0fd1de320d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105086} {:answer/id #uuid "4676739b-d412-5a49-9318-b1f21577b915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105087} {:answer/id #uuid "49e19359-e41d-5a98-ac49-e62cc96b337a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11146513-ee02-52e2-a190-e0fd1de320d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105086} :answer-field/id #uuid "f03d2249-cafd-515b-91b9-adcc4fb91865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54e9af2f-d595-580f-bd22-59bd51ee5360" :question/math-academy-id "q-83457" :question/problem "| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X\\in {\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}})$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\in {\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}}) & =P(X=\\frac{1}{2})+P(X=\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{4}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df767911-20d5-5987-b68e-e6b431632cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359710} {:answer/id #uuid "199438ba-f257-5cf8-8d61-7ad34062a6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359711} {:answer/id #uuid "808c4f90-8eed-502e-94fd-5224476d06de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359712} {:answer/id #uuid "50053a91-466d-53bf-aacf-4333fd328edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359713} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c319e0-1b31-5631-8176-311bffb7869c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "808c4f90-8eed-502e-94fd-5224476d06de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359712} :answer-field/id #uuid "1496f1b3-3206-5295-8157-b89b4a39a8e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5e4eba9-5bfb-5f59-b576-7c3746e63e3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83762" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of seats in a randomly selected sports stadium\n- The volume of milk produced by a cow on a particular day\n- The distance that a particular sprinter can run in $1$ minute" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, we cannot list the possible real-numbered values in a sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35193ec5-030d-5c78-b536-08e23bd3b7d4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within a Set of Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49ca48db-ad11-53d3-a997-946b94da5591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359717} {:answer/id #uuid "897bfd93-5466-52a6-b1af-2acc7f316b0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359718} {:answer/id #uuid "5395e9fd-9dc2-5889-9a1e-ea81947f73c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359719} {:answer/id #uuid "088a7a34-08eb-5e97-9462-edf1062e4f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359720} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f55a96-80f0-5531-8936-352bddc0d3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "897bfd93-5466-52a6-b1af-2acc7f316b0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359718} :answer-field/id #uuid "64f01fe2-a3cf-5aa0-a337-680f1fec1cf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30e2fc94-49a4-5a60-a70c-3f0f0020a8e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-83773" :question/problem "| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n\nCompute $P(X\\in {2,6,8})$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\in {2,6,8}) & =P(X=2)+P(X=6)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =0.2+0.2+0.3 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e34ce770-b39e-5d0c-9393-a3ed721062bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359724} {:answer/id #uuid "bae4acea-53aa-5531-887a-9b61975cf565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359725} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e14f29-e397-5474-884c-c13225b7b143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359726} {:answer/id #uuid "920036b6-38e0-5cbd-ac05-dc6ad4e7179b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359727} {:answer/id #uuid "60192fa6-ead5-5a8d-a808-51c0fd61c553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60192fa6-ead5-5a8d-a808-51c0fd61c553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359728} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbbda390-c761-55b7-95e9-53ab79698d3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "983a5e02-e77d-5884-ae2d-ea4939ca0553" :question/math-academy-id "q-89206" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The exact time (not rounded to integers) that a full charge of a phone battery lasts\n- The number of pets owned by a randomly selected family in a town\n- The height of a randomly selected plant in a nursery" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does not describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,….$\n\n- Statement III does not describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
